<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

	<!--
		DR-04 - LONG PATH AWARENESS, DECLARED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.

		DR-04 requires the binary to declare long-path awareness, because without it a deep file
		is skipped on one platform and analyzed on another, which breaks determinism of the SET
		of findings and not merely of their order. Only Windows imposes the limit this answers,
		and only Windows reads this file.

		WHY A MANIFEST IS THE LEVER, AND WHY NO MSBUILD PROPERTY IS. There is no property named
		after long paths. The switch is an operating-system one: a process declares itself
		long-path aware in its application manifest, and Windows then honours paths past MAX_PATH
		for that process where the registry policy is also enabled. Neither the .NET SDK nor the
		native apphost supplies a manifest carrying it - checked, in this SDK, on this machine -
		so a binary that does not name it does not have it.

		HOW IT REACHES A NATIVE AOT BINARY, which is the part that is not obvious. The AOT link
		step passes /MANIFEST:NO, so nothing is generated at link time; what carries it instead
		is the compiler. ApplicationManifest hands this file to the C# compiler, which embeds it
		in the managed assembly's Win32 resources, and the AOT compiler is invoked with its
		win32resourcemodule option pointing at that assembly, which copies those resources into the
		native image. The declaration therefore survives into the published single-file binary
		by the same route its version information does.

		requestedExecutionLevel is asInvoker and is here for a second reason of its own: this
		tool must never be silently elevated, because what it can read is what it reports, and
		DR-21 reads the requirement's "equivalent repository and file layout" as including an
		equivalent running identity.

		XML forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a comment. The line
		above once named the compiler option with its two-hyphen prefix, which made this whole
		file malformed XML. Windows refuses to create an activation context from a malformed
		manifest, so neither the framework-dependent apphost nor the Native AOT binary could
		start. Keep every comment in this file free of a double hyphen.
	-->

	<trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
		<security>
			<requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
				<requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
			</requestedPrivileges>
		</security>
	</trustInfo>

	<application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
		<windowsSettings>
			<longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
		</windowsSettings>
	</application>

</assembly>
